In C++, a constructor is defined as follows:
class MyClass {
private:
    int member1;  // First declared member
    int member2;  // Second declared member
public:
    MyClass(int val1, int val2) : member2(val2), member1(val1) {  // Initialization list; compiler may reorder to member1 first, then member2
        // User code here; compiler inserts initialization code before this
    }
};
Implementation notes: The compiler reorders the initialization list to match declaration order (e.g., member1 before member2), generates code for base class initialization if inherited, and ensures vtable pointers are correctly set for virtual functions. For virtual inheritance, the compiler handles virtual base pointers in the memory layout.
